      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ramapo’s first inhabitants were the Munsee, a band of the Lenape nation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramapo%2C_New_York",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town's name, recorded variously as Ramopuck, Ramapock, or Ramapough, is of Lenape origin, meaning either 'sweet water' or 'slanting rocks'.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramapo%2C_New_York",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first railroad line across Rockland County was built in 1841 and ran from Piermont to Ramapo.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramapo%2C_New_York",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Ramapo’s Harmony Hall, noted for its architecture, earned a spot on the State and National Historic Registers in 2006.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
